2026-09-08T08:02Z claude note Found by following the author's live journal on doudou during the 2026-09-08 pre-release review, not by reading code. The census reported 406 "could not be indexed", which is what sent me looking; the number turned out to conflate two unrelated facts, and the residue under it is this defect. Author's rulings, same session, verbatim in substance: (1) "ne pas croire l'etiquette, le B A BA du consommateur averti"; (2) "oui on persiste en cache, jetable c'est un cache"; (3) the census categories are to be separated in the dialog's account, which is filed against 0693 rather than here.

--- body ---
## Context

The sitter's census reports a single figure, "Could not be indexed (last
census): 406". Read on the author's live library on 2026-09-08 it decomposes,
and the classes sum exactly to the scanned total:

    current           13 699
    unsupported        2 600
    missing-source       367
    failed-session        39
    excluded               1
                      ------
    scanned           16 706

So **367 of the 406 are `missing-source`** — the file is not on this disk,
which is an ordinary fact about a synced library and not a failure of anything.
Only **39 are real failures**, and they are what this ticket is about. The
conflation is not cosmetic: it sent this session's own analysis the wrong way
twice before the breakdown was read, which is the argument for the separated
account filed against 0693.

## What the 39 are

The live journal (`bench/results/sdt-sitter-2026-09-08/0.3.5-all-admissions-fail.json`)
names twelve of them by cache key. Resolved against `~/data/Zotero/storage` and
matched by byte size to the `admit` records — every size matches to the digit,
so the mapping is measured and not assumed:

- **Eleven are JPEG images**, page scans of historical works: `Cantor1896-p161
  .jpg`, `-p162`, `-p163`, `Westergaard1890-p220.jpg`, `Sincov1904-p216.jpg`,
  `-p217`, `Frisch1930-p398.jpg`, `-p401`, `-p403`, `Frisch1936-p7.jpg`,
  `Frege1874-p5.jpg`. `file(1)` confirms real `JPEG image data, JFIF standard
  1.01` bytes.
- **One is a genuine HTML snapshot**, `Shananin2009-sciup-snapshot.html`, real
  UTF-8 HTML. Its failure has a DIFFERENT and still unknown cause and must not
  be folded into the image story.

Every failing admission carries `pages: null`, and each takes 50–90 ms from
`admit` to `settle` — far too fast to be extraction trying and losing. The error
is the plugin's own sentence, not a platform exception: `Native SDT did not
persist a current pack`.

## Why an image reaches a text extractor

`inspect()` (bootstrap.js:1259) classifies by the host's own predicates:

    const processor = item.isPDFAttachment() ? 'pdf'
      : item.isEPUBAttachment() ? 'epub'
      : item.isSnapshotAttachment() ? 'snapshot' : null;
    if (!processor) return { status: 'unsupported' };

Read in the installed build (`chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/data/item.js`),
`isSnapshotAttachment()` is

    this.attachmentLinkMode == LINK_MODE_IMPORTED_URL &&
    this.attachmentContentType == 'text/html'

which a JPEG cannot satisfy. Since these items WERE admitted, and `snapshot` is
the only class they could have entered under, **Zotero records these JPEGs with
`attachmentContentType == 'text/html'`**. That step is a deduction from the code
rather than a direct read — `zotero.sqlite` is locked while Zotero runs — but
the code leaves no other path, and it is consistent with how the files were
acquired: a captured page that served an image.

So the plugin trusts a label that is wrong about the bytes underneath it. Native
SDT is handed a document declared HTML whose content is a photograph, finds no
text, persists no pack, and returns. The platform is behaving correctly
throughout — 4 729 of this library's 4 890 snapshot directories DO carry a pack,
so snapshots as a class are handled fine.

## Why it repeats forever

`failed-session` is exactly what its name says: the exclusion lasts the session.
Next activation, the same 39 documents are candidates again, are admitted again,
fail again in the same 50–90 ms, and are counted again. They can never succeed,
and nothing remembers that.

## What this does to ticket 0606

0606 turns on how many documents native extraction genuinely cannot handle,
since that is the case for producing packs ourselves. On the evidence here the
answer is smaller than any figure so far quoted: eleven of twelve sampled
failures are images that a text extractor is right to refuse. They argue for not
submitting them, not for building a producer. Ticket 0728 carries the residue
recount that reaches the same place from the other direction.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `inspect()` at :1254, the processor
  classification at :1259
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `failed-session` and the candidate queue
- `bench/results/sdt-sitter-2026-09-08/` — the two live journals this rests on
- `/opt/zotero7/app/omni.ja`, `chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/data/item.js` — the
  three `is*Attachment()` predicates as the running build defines them

## Actions

1. **Do not trust the declared type.** Before admitting, confirm the bytes are
   what the label claims — a magic-number read of the first few bytes is enough
   to separate a JPEG from HTML and costs one short read per candidate, once,
   against a document that is otherwise about to be handed to a worker. A
   mismatch is `unsupported`, not a failure: the document is fine, our
   classification of it was wrong.
2. **Persist the refusal.** When a document is admitted and native returns
   without a current pack, remember that in the on-disk cache against the
   document's identity, so the next session does not retry it. Author's ruling:
   the cache is the right home precisely because it is disposable — a wrong
   entry costs one re-attempt after a cache reset, which is the correct price
   for being wrong. The identity already embeds the source hash and the pack
   versions, so a re-saved file or a native version bump re-opens the question
   by itself, which is exactly the invalidation this needs.
3. Keep the genuine HTML snapshot out of this fix and out of its story. One
   real snapshot failing is a separate question; record it and leave it.

## Test

- Red first, for action 1: an attachment whose declared type is `text/html` and
  whose bytes are a JPEG must be classified `unsupported`, and must never reach
  `submit`. Against the current tree it is admitted and fails, so the test
  reddens today. The mock already carries the seams for a fixture of this shape.
- Red first, for action 2: a document that fails with "no current pack" must not
  be a candidate on the NEXT activation — drive two activations over one cache
  and assert the second admits nothing. Note the trap: asserting "it failed
  again" passes against both the fixed and unfixed code. Assert that it was
  never admitted.
- The mixed-population trap is the one to watch on any count assertion here: a
  test whose fixture holds only failures cannot see a fix that also stops
  admitting the good documents.

## Verification

- [ ] A byte/label mismatch is classified `unsupported` and never submitted
- [ ] A "no current pack" outcome survives a restart and is not retried
- [ ] Both tests reddened against the unfixed tree before the fix
- [ ] The census total still equals the sum of its classes on the live library
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- The plugin still never writes to `storage/`, never changes extraction
  settings, and never replaces a source attachment.
- A document is never permanently written off on a ground that can change: the
  persisted refusal is keyed on the identity, so a re-saved source or a native
  version bump re-admits it.
- Native SDT is not second-guessed. This ticket stops us handing it work it
  cannot do; it does not add extraction of our own.

## Exit criteria

Images declared as HTML are classified rather than submitted, a document that
cannot yield a pack is asked once rather than every session, and both are held
by tests that were red first.
